{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000caa5","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0a9c5-2eb3-7902-83b0-77aeb115d140"},"sequence":941,"recorded_at":1780531400001877,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"0bd83bfa-ae0a-4313-a4d0-115d5a67d246","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"0bd83bfa-ae0a-4313-a4d0-115d5a67d246","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"0bd83bfa-ae0a-4313-a4d0-115d5a67d246"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0a9cb-2045-75b1-bd89-864fb6754eba"},"task_id":"01a0a9cb-2045-75b1-bd89-864fb6754eba","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0a9cb-2045-75b1-bd89-864fb6754eba","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Documents/Codex/2026-09-15/new-chat-4/work/skills/skill-ios-testing-tools/agents/skills/ios-testing-tools/SKILL.md`.\n1|---\n2|name: ios-testing-tools\n3|description: |\n4|  iOS UI testing toolkit with screenshot validation. Use when:\n5|  (1) Setting up UI test infrastructure with Page Object pattern\n6|  (2) Creating accessibility identifiers with structured naming\n7|  (3) Writing UI tests with step-by-step screenshots\n8|  (4) Validating UI via screenshot comparison\n9|  (5) Writing snapshot tests with swift-snapshot-testing\n10|  (6) Comparing snapshot diffs with snapshotsdiff CLI\n11|  (7) Integrating with Allure for test reporting\n12|  (8) Organizing shared test identifiers between app and test targets\n13|  (9) Building physical iOS/iOS E2E harnesses with XCUITest runner-app marker synchronization\n14|  File types: Swift UI tests, XCUITest, xcresult, Allure reports, snapshot tests\n15|---\n16|\n17|# iOS UI Validation\n18|\n19|Write UI, add UI tests with structured markup, validate via screenshots.\n20|\n21|> **Repository:** [swift-ui-testing-tools](https://github.com/ivalx1s/swift-ui-testing-tools)\n22|>\n23|> **Before using this skill, check for updates:**\n24|> ```bash\n25|> cd ~/src/swift-ui-testing-tools && git pull\n26|> ```\n27|\n28|## Prerequisites\n29|\n30|**Before starting, verify required tools are installed:**\n31|\n32|```bash\n33|./Scripts/check-tools.sh\n34|```\n35|\n36|Required:\n37|- Xcode Command Line Tools (`xcodebuild`, `xcrun`, `xcresulttool`)\n38|- Swift 5.9+\n39|- iOS Simulator with available devices when the selected test platform is iOS\n40|\n41|## Test Platform Selection\n42|\n43|Pick the execution platform from the target declaration before running anything.\n44|\n45|- iOS app target or app package: run tests and runtime by default on iOS Simulator.\n46|- App target or app package on another declared platform: run against that declared platform.\n47|- If the app/package does not declare any platform, default to macOS.\n48|- Package module or multi-platform package: run unit and integration tests on macOS when macOS is supported.\n49|- If macOS is not supported, prefer iOS when iOS is declared.\n50|- If neither macOS nor iOS is available, use the first declared platform.\n51|- Use simulator destinations only for iOS-family runs. For macOS, run on macOS directly.\n52|- If a reusable iOS package is consumed through an app-local graph and standalone package schemes cannot resolve local dependencies, verify it through the app workspace/test host instead of treating the standalone package build as the product build.\n53|\n54|## Native macOS without UI tests\n55|\n56|When the user excludes UI tests, honor that scope: use Swift Testing for reducers, services and integration boundaries. Do not scaffold XCUITest, require screenshot extraction from unit-only runs, or launch iOS simulators. Use `Scripts/run-macos-package-tests.sh --package <CorePackage> --output <project>/.temp/macos-tests` from this repository. It preserves logs, JUnit output and the failure exit code. Hostless Core packages keep menu-bar app lifecycle out of the tests. For launchd controllers, test with a unique temporary service and harmless process; always unload it, including after failures. Never start production CI jobs as a unit-test side effect.\n57|\n58|A native menu-bar app needs native macOS UI, not the Designed for iPad destination. Build the app with `-destination 'platform=macOS'`, inspect its signed bundle and launch the app normally for manual smoke validation. Do not mistake local service state for GitHub online/busy state. Keep app-level runtime verification separate from automated UI tests.\n59|\n60|## Apple Silicon Mac iOS-App Runtime\n61|\n62|Use this path when the product app is an iOS app and the test environment needs a Mac-side peer or host process on Apple Silicon. This is **not** a separate macOS app target and not Catalyst. It is the existing iPhoneOS app running on the Mac as `Designed for iPad/iPhone`.\n63|\n64|First verify that Xcode exposes the destination:\n65|\n66|```bash\n67|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n68|xcodebuild \\\n69|  -workspace App.xcworkspace \\\n70|  -scheme App \\\n71|  -showdestinations\n72|```\n73|\n74|Look for a destination like:\n75|\n76|```text\n77|{ platform:macOS, arch:arm64, variant:Designed for [iPad,iPhone], name:My Mac }\n78|```\n79|\n80|Build the existing iOS app for that destination:\n81|\n82|```bash\n83|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n84|xcodebuild \\\n85|  -workspace App.xcworkspace \\\n86|  -scheme App \\\n87|  -destination 'platform=macOS,arch=arm64,variant=Designed for iPad' \\\n88|  build\n89|```\n90|\n91|Important limitations:\n92|\n93|- Do not create a separate macOS app target just to get a Mac-side peer. Use the existing iOS app on the Apple Silicon Mac destination unless the product explicitly needs native macOS UI.\n94|- `open Debug-iphoneos/App.app` is not a valid launch path for this runtime. macOS may report `incorrect executable format` because the product is still an iPhoneOS bundle.\n95|- `devicectl` targets physical devices; it does not reliably expose the Apple Silicon Mac `Designed for iPad` runtime as an install/launch device.\n96|- XCUITest cannot drive the Mac `Designed for iPad` destination. `xcodebuild test` can fail with `UI tests are not supported on My Mac (Designed for iPad)`.\n97|\n98|To launch the Mac-hosted iOS app from automation, ask Xcode to run the scheme through AppleScript. This uses the same run machinery as pressing Run in Xcode:\n99|\n100|```bash\n101|osascript <<'APPLESCRIPT'\n102|set workspacePath to POSIX file \"/absolute/path/App.xcworkspace\"\n103|tell application \"Xcode\"\n104|    activate\n105|    open workspacePath\n106|    set workspaceDocument to workspace document \"App.xcworkspace\"\n107|    repeat 120 times\n108|        if loaded of workspaceDocument is true then exit repeat\n109|        delay 0.5\n110|    end repeat\n111|    if loaded of workspaceDocument is false then error \"Xcode workspace did not finish loading\"\n112|    set actionResult to debug workspaceDocument scheme \"App\" run destination specifier \"platform=macOS,arch=arm64,variant=Designed for iPad\" skip building true\n113|    return id of actionResult\n114|end tell\n115|APPLESCRIPT\n116|```\n117|\n118|Stop the running action when the test environment is done:\n119|\n120|```bash\n121|osascript <<'APPLESCRIPT'\n122|tell application \"Xcode\"\n123|    set workspaceDocument to workspace document \"App.xcworkspace\"\n124|    stop workspaceDocument\n125|end tell\n126|APPLESCRIPT\n127|```\n128|\n129|For cross-runtime e2e, run the Mac-hosted iOS app as one endpoint, then run the actual XCUITest against the physical iPhone or Simulator endpoint:\n130|\n131|```bash\n132|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n133|xcodebuild \\\n134|  -workspace App.xcworkspace \\\n135|  -scheme AppUITests \\\n136|  -destination 'id=<physical-device-udid>' \\\n137|  -only-testing:AppUITests/SomeE2ETests/testPhoneSeesMacHostedPeer \\\n138|  test\n139|```\n140|\n141|Keep screenshots and assertions on the XCTest-driven endpoint. For the Mac-hosted iOS app, rely on app automation configuration, process checks, Xcode action status, logs, or manual permission approval when the OS prompts for Bluetooth, Local Network, or similar privacy access.\n142|\n143|## Connected Device Builds\n144|\n145|Use `ios-device-build` from this skill source repo when an iOS app should be built for every currently connected physical device without hard-coding an expected device list.\n146|\n147|The CLI has an explicit contract: always pass the Xcode source (`--workspace` or `--project`), `--scheme`, `--targets`, and `--derived-data-root`. Pass `--discovery` whenever `--targets` includes physical iPhones/iPads. Do not rely on inferred scheme names, inferred workspace/project names, or default discovery modes. The tool fails on missing required parameters so the agent can see exactly which assumption was wrong.\n148|\n149|The source repo path used by the local workflow is:\n150|\n151|```bash\n152|TOOLKIT=/Users/alexis/src/relux-works/skill-ios-testing-tools\n153|```\n154|\n155|### Physical Device Build Means Install\n156|\n157|When the user asks to build \"on\", \"for\", or \"to\" a physical iPhone/iPad, treat the request as **build a signed app bundle and install it on the target device** unless the user explicitly asks for compile-only validation.\n158|\n159|`xcodebuild build -destination 'platform=iOS,id=<udid>'` only proves that the app can compile and sign for that device class. It does not update the app installed on the phone. After a successful physical-device build, install the resulting `.app` with `devicectl device install app --device <udid> <path-to-app>` or a project-provided build/install wrapper, then verify the install command succeeded. Launch the app as a separate step only when the user asks to run it or when runtime validation is part of the task.\n160|\n161|Build only cable-connected physical iPhones/iPads:\n162|\n163|```bash\n164|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n165|$TOOLKIT/Scripts/ios-device-build.sh \\\n166|  --workspace App.xcworkspace \\\n167|  --scheme App \\\n168|  --discovery usb \\\n169|  --targets iphones \\\n170|  --derived-data-root .temp/device-builds\n171|```\n172|\n173|Build every physical iOS device Xcode sees over USB or Wi-Fi:\n174|\n175|```bash\n176|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n177|$TOOLKIT/Scripts/ios-device-build.sh \\\n178|  --project App.xcodeproj \\\n179|  --scheme App \\\n180|  --discovery usb,wifi \\\n181|  --targets iphones \\\n182|  --derived-data-root .temp/device-builds\n183|```\n184|\n185|Build physical iOS devices plus the Apple Silicon Mac `Designed for iPad/iPhone` destination:\n186|\n187|```bash\n188|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n189|$TOOLKIT/Scripts/ios-device-build.sh \\\n190|  --workspace App.xcworkspace \\\n191|  --scheme App \\\n192|  --discovery usb \\\n193|  --targets iphones,macbook \\\n194|  --mac-destination 'platform=macOS,arch=arm64,variant=Designed for iPad' \\\n195|  --derived-data-root .temp/device-builds\n196|```\n197|\n198|Build only the Apple Silicon Mac `Designed for iPad/iPhone` destination:\n199|\n200|```bash\n201|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n202|$TOOLKIT/Scripts/ios-device-build.sh \\\n203|  --workspace App.xcworkspace \\\n204|  --scheme App \\\n205|  --targets macbook \\\n206|  --mac-destination 'platform=macOS,arch=arm64,variant=Designed for iPad' \\\n207|  --derived-data-root .temp/device-builds\n208|```\n209|\n210|Rules:\n211|\n212|- `--discovery` is an option set for physical-device interfaces: `usb`, `wifi`, or `usb,wifi`.\n213|- `--discovery` is required only when `--targets` includes `iphones`.\n214|- `--targets` is a destination-type set: `iphones`, `macbook`, or `iphones,macbook`.\n215|- Prefer `--discovery usb` for stable cable-connected work; Wi-Fi destinations can be stale or unstable.\n216|- Missing individual devices do not fail the command; the CLI builds whatever matching destinations are discovered.\n217|- No matching destinations is a failure.\n218|- Every planned destination gets its own DerivedData and `xcodebuild.log` under the required `--derived-data-root`.\n219|- Use `--dry-run` first when debugging discovery.\n220|\n221|## Physical Device Runtime Logs\n222|\n223|When UI tests or runtime behavior are investigated on a physical iPhone, inspect the live device console instead of guessing from `xcodebuild` output alone.\n224|\n225|- Prefer `devicectl device process launch --console` for app-scoped runtime logs on a connected device.\n226|- Always set `DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer` before `xcrun` commands if the shell does not already point at Xcode.\n227|- Resolve the target device first with `xcrun xcdevice list`.\n228|- Use the device UDID in commands; names can be ambiguous.\n229|\n230|Recommended flow:\n231|\n232|```bash\n233|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n234|xcrun xcdevice list\n235|```\n236|\n237|```bash\n238|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n239|xcrun devicectl device process launch \\\n240|  --device <device-udid> \\\n241|  --terminate-existing \\\n242|  --console \\\n243|  <bundle-id>\n244|```\n245|\n246|Notes:\n247|- `--console` attaches stdout/stderr and waits for app exit, so use it for live runtime triage.\n248|- `--terminate-existing` avoids reading stale logs from an older app process.\n249|- This is the preferred path for investigating auth/network/runtime failures on device.\n250|- If you need broader system logs beyond one app process, fall back to Xcode Devices window or Console.app with the same physical device selected.\n251|\n252|## Simulator Runtime Logs\n253|\n254|When debugging simulator runtime behavior, use `simctl spawn` with unified logging and keep the raw stream in `.temp/` for later grepping.\n255|\n256|First resolve and boot the simulator:\n257|\n258|```bash\n259|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n260|xcrun simctl list devices available\n261|\n262|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n263|xcrun simctl boot <simulator-udid> 2>/dev/null || true\n264|\n265|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n266|xcrun simctl bootstatus <simulator-udid> -b\n267|```\n268|\n269|Start a task-scoped log stream before launching the app or UI test:\n270|\n271|```bash\n272|mkdir -p .temp/<task-id>\n273|\n274|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n275|xcrun simctl spawn <simulator-udid> \\\n276|  log stream \\\n277|  --style compact \\\n278|  --level debug \\\n279|  --predicate 'process == \"AppName\" OR subsystem CONTAINS \"AppName\" OR category CONTAINS \"Auth\" OR eventMessage CONTAINS \"SDKSSO\"' \\\n280|  > .temp/<task-id>/sim-runtime-01.log 2>&1 &\n281|\n282|echo $! > .temp/<task-id>/sim-runtime-01.pid\n283|```\n284|\n285|Launch the app or run the UI test while the stream is active:\n286|\n287|```bash\n288|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n289|xcrun simctl launch --terminate-running-process \\\n290|  <simulator-udid> \\\n291|  <bundle-id>\n292|```\n293|\n294|Search the captured stream with focused patterns:\n295|\n296|```bash\n297|rg -n \"SDKSSO|Auth|login\\\\.mts|client|scope|redirect|callback|error|failed|exception\" \\\n298|  .temp/<task-id>/sim-runtime-01.log\n299|```\n300|\n301|Stop the stream after triage:\n302|\n303|```bash\n304|kill \"$(cat .temp/<task-id>/sim-runtime-01.pid)\" 2>/dev/null || true\n305|```\n306|\n307|If the app writes file diagnostics, resolve the simulator data container and inspect it directly:\n308|\n309|```bash\n310|CONTAINER=\"$(DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n311|  xcrun simctl get_app_container <simulator-udid> <bundle-id> data)\"\n312|\n313|find \"$CONTAINER/Documents\" -maxdepth 4 -type f -print\n314|```\n315|\n316|Notes:\n317|- Use unified logs for SDK/framework logs, `os_log`, and app runtime events.\n318|- Use the app Documents container for explicit file diagnostics such as CSV logs.\n319|- Do not rely on `xcodebuild` output alone; it often misses the failing runtime line.\n320|- Keep logs under `.temp/` and name them with the task id plus a sequence number.\n321|\n322|## Physical Device Tunnel Recovery\n323|\n324|Use this playbook when Xcode or `devicectl` can see a physical iPhone, pairing succeeds, but developer operations fail with a tunnel error such as:\n325|\n326|```text\n327|The tunnel connection failed while the system tried to connect to the device.\n328|Domain: com.apple.dt.CoreDeviceError\n329|Code: 4\n330|--\n331|Domain: com.apple.dt.RemotePairingError\n332|Code: 4\n333|```\n334|\n335|Typical `devicectl device info details` symptoms:\n336|\n337|- `pairingState: paired`\n338|- `transportType: wired` or `localNetwork`\n339|- `tunnelState: disconnected`\n340|- `ddiServicesAvailable: false`\n341|- `devicectl device info lockState --device <udid>` fails with `RemotePairingError Code=4`\n342|\n343|First collect the state and compare with a known-good physical device if one is attached:\n344|\n345|```bash\n346|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n347|xcrun xcdevice list\n348|\n349|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n350|xcrun devicectl list devices\n351|\n352|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n353|xcrun devicectl device info details \\\n354|  --device <device-udid> \\\n355|  --verbose \\\n356|  --timeout 30 \\\n357|  --json-output .temp/device-details.json \\\n358|  --log-output .temp/device-details.log\n359|\n360|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n361|xcrun devicectl device info lockState \\\n362|  --device <device-udid> \\\n363|  --verbose \\\n364|  --timeout 30 \\\n365|  --json-output .temp/device-lockstate.json \\\n366|  --log-output .temp/device-lockstate.log\n367|```\n368|\n369|If the device charges but does not appear in `ioreg`, the Mac only sees power, not the USB data interface. Reboot the iPhone, unlock it, reconnect a known-good data cable/port, and accept the Trust prompt before spending time on Xcode caches:\n370|\n371|```bash\n372|ioreg -p IOUSB -l -w 0 | rg \"USB Serial Number|<device-udid-without-dashes>\"\n373|```\n374|\n375|If USB data and pairing are present but the tunnel still fails, use the iPhone-side Developer networking reset before clearing host caches:\n376|\n377|1. On the iPhone, open `Settings -> Developer -> Networking`.\n378|2. Enable `Network Link Conditioner`.\n379|3. If present, enable `Network Override`; it may be absent on some iPhone/iOS builds.\n380|4. Open `Responsiveness`.\n381|5. Run `Test Responsiveness` and wait for it to finish.\n382|6. Retry `devicectl device info lockState --device <device-udid>`.\n383|\n384|This can recover a stale Developer networking stack without rebooting, unpairing, or resetting trusted computers. A successful recovery usually changes `details` to:\n385|\n386|- `tunnelState: connected`\n387|- `ddiServicesAvailable: true`\n388|- `developerModeStatus: enabled`\n389|- a non-empty `tunnelIPAddress`\n390|\n391|If the issue persists after the Responsiveness test:\n392|\n393|- Disable `Multipath Networking` in `Settings -> Developer` if the setting exists.\n394|- Check for host VPN or network filter interfaces with `scutil --nwi` and `ifconfig | rg \"utun|ipsec|tap|tun\"`.\n395|- Restart CoreDevice host services and retry:\n396|\n397|```bash\n398|killall CoreDeviceService remoted 2>/dev/null || true\n399|\n400|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n401|xcrun devicectl manage ddis update --verbose --timeout 120\n402|```\n403|\n404|- If the iPhone OS is newer than the installed Xcode support, install a matching Xcode/device support bundle before continuing.\n405|- Only then consider heavier steps such as unpairing, resetting Location & Privacy/trusted computers, deleting Xcode/CoreDevice caches, or rebooting the Mac.\n406|\n407|## Physical iOS/iOS E2E Synchronization\n408|\n409|For paired physical-device tests where two iPhones must coordinate roles such as observing peer, controlled peer, project-specific discovery signals, project-specific state transitions, or session handoff, use reactive orchestration instead of fixed sleeps.\n410|\n411|Read [references/physical-ios-ios-e2e-sync.md](references/physical-ios-ios-e2e-sync.md) before implementing or debugging a two-device harness.\n412|\n413|For XCUITest runner-app marker files, host-side cross-device marker bridges, and screenshot/snapshot evidence contracts, also read [references/physical-ios-ios-runner-marker-sync.md](references/physical-ios-ios-runner-marker-sync.md).\n414|\n415|## Generalized WebSocket E2E Coordinator Sample\n416|\n417|Use the toolkit-local sample smoke to validate the reusable WebSocket coordinator, process peer runner, event log, receipt log, replay path, `acked` delivery barrier, JSON payload assertions, timestamp envelope, and deterministic artifact layout without any consumer product project or physical device.\n418|\n419|Run from the toolkit source repo:\n420|\n421|```bash\n422|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n423|./Scripts/run-e2e-sample-smoke.sh\n424|```\n425|\n426|The smoke writes artifacts under `.temp/e2e-sample/<session-id>/` and fails if the required events, receipts, peer markers, summary, or logs are missing.\n427|\n428|For physical devices, the XCUITest runner process must be able to open a TCP connection to the coordinator. Use global `coordinator.advertisedHost` when all peers share the same reachable host. Use peer-level `coordinatorHost` when different peers need different routes to the same coordinator; the runner keeps the global bind/port/path and injects a peer-specific `E2E_COORDINATOR_URL`.\n429|\n430|```yaml\n431|peers:\n432|  - name: peer-a\n433|    coordinatorHost: 192.168.50.10\n434|    launch:\n435|      kind: xctest\n436|      startWhen:\n437|        type: immediate\n438|```\n439|\n440|## Generalized Peer-Listener E2E Transport\n441|\n442|Use `coordinator.transport: peer-listener` when physical iOS peers cannot reach a Mac-hosted coordinator directly. The UI test peer starts a device-side TCP listener through `UITestE2EClient.fromEnvironment()`, the Mac runner starts `iproxy` for each device, and the Mac coordinator connects to every peer through host-local forwarded ports.\n443|\n444|This transport is project-neutral. Keep all product scenario semantics in the consumer project's UI tests and config. Do not add consumer-specific events, device names, bundle IDs, or business flow assumptions to this toolkit or skill.\n445|\n446|Consumer UI tests should import `IOSE2EPeerClient`, not `IOSE2ECoordinatorCore`, for generalized E2E coordination. Keep `UITestKit` for common UI helpers. Use `client.environment.peerNameValue` for scenario branching and `UITestE2EClient.publish` / `waitFor` for JSON event synchronization.\n447|\n448|Run the toolkit-local peer-listener smoke before attaching a product project:\n449|\n450|```bash\n451|DEVELOPER_DIR=/Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer \\\n452|./Scripts/run-e2e-peer-listener-sample-smoke.sh\n453|```\n454|\n455|Minimal config shape:\n456|\n457|```yaml\n458|coordinator:\n459|  transport: peer-listener\n460|\n461|peers:\n462|  - name: peer-a\n463|    launch:\n464|      kind: xctest\n465|      startWhen:\n466|        type: immediate\n467|    connection:\n468|      listenPort: 19131\n469|      connectHost: 127.0.0.1\n470|      connectPort: 18131\n471|      proxy:\n472|        kind: iproxy\n473|        udid: 00000000-0000000000000000\n474|```\n475|\n476|If a physical run fails before peer logs are written, first check CoreDevice state. A peer with `tunnelState: disconnected` or `ddiServicesAvailable: false` cannot reliably launch XCUITest even when `xcdevice list` says `available=true`.\n477|\n478|## Main Workflow\n479|\n480|1. **Write UI** with accessibility identifiers\n481|2. **Write UI tests** using Page Object pattern\n482|3. **Capture screenshots** at each step\n483|4. **Extract and review** screenshots to verify UI looks as expected\n484|5. **MANDATORY: Visually verify screenshots** - open and check that UI is rendered correctly\n485|\n486|## CRITICAL: Screenshot Verification\n487|\n488|After extracting screenshots, **ALWAYS open and visually inspect them** using the Read tool. Check for:\n489|\n490|- **Orientation issues** - UI should not be rotated/sideways\n491|- **Layout problems** - UI elements should be visible and properly positioned, not squished in corners\n492|- **Content visibility** - all expected UI elements should be present and readable\n493|- **Black screens** - indicates app didn't launch or UI didn't render\n494|\n495|Common issues and fixes:\n496|- **Rotated UI in landscape frame**: Force portrait in PageManager: `XCUIDevice.shared.orientation = .portrait`\n497|- **Empty/black screenshots**: Check app launch, add delays if needed\n498|- **Partial UI**: Scroll or adjust viewport before screenshot\n499|\n500|## Core Components\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000caa7","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0a9c5-2eb3-7902-83b0-77aeb115d140"},"sequence":942,"recorded_at":1780531400001879,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"0bd83bfa-ae0a-4313-a4d0-115d5a67d246","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"0bd83bfa-ae0a-4313-a4d0-115d5a67d246","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"0bd83bfa-ae0a-4313-a4d0-115d5a67d246"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0a9cb-2045-75b1-bd89-864fb6754eba"},"task_id":"01a0a9cb-2045-75b1-bd89-864fb6754eba","event":{"kind":"completed","task_id":"01a0a9cb-2045-75b1-bd89-864fb6754eba"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000caa9","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0a9c5-2eb3-7902-83b0-77aeb115d140"},"sequence":943,"recorded_at":1780531400001881,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"0bd83bfa-ae0a-4313-a4d0-115d5a67d246","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"0bd83bfa-ae0a-4313-a4d0-115d5a67d246","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"0bd83bfa-ae0a-4313-a4d0-115d5a67d246"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0a9cb-1fbc-7233-9ae4-b9f29459f461"},"task_id":"01a0a9cb-1fbc-7233-9ae4-b9f29459f461","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0a9cb-1fbc-7233-9ae4-b9f29459f461","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Documents/Codex/2026-09-15/new-chat-4/work/skills/skill-ios-app-manager/SKILL.md`.\n1|---\n2|name: ios-app-manager\n3|description: iOS app project management CLI for Tuist-based projects with Relux architecture\n4|triggers:\n5|  - ios project\n6|  - tuist\n7|  - relux module\n8|  - ios-app-manager\n9|globs:\n10|  - ios-app-manager.json\n11|  - \"**/Tuist/**\"\n12|---\n13|\n14|# ios-app-manager\n15|\n16|Agent-facing workflow for managing Tuist-based iOS projects that follow Relux module conventions.\n17|\n18|## Setup\n19|\n20|```bash\n21|# Clone and run setup (builds CLI, installs runtime skill copy, symlinks binary)\n22|git clone git@github.com:relux-works/skill-ios-app-manager.git\n23|cd skill-ios-app-manager\n24|./scripts/setup.sh\n25|\n26|# Optional one-time full Xcode selection for iOS simulator/build/profile workflows\n27|./scripts/setup.sh --print-privileged-commands\n28|./scripts/setup.sh --select-xcode\n29|```\n30|\n31|Setup does:\n32|- Builds Go CLI binary in `tuist-starter/`\n33|- Copies the skill repo into `~/.agents/skills/ios-app-manager`\n34|- Degitizes the installed copy after sync (`.git`, `.gitignore`, `.gitattributes`, `.gitmodules` are removed)\n35|- Symlinks `~/.claude/skills/ios-app-manager` and `~/.codex/skills/ios-app-manager` to the installed copy in `~/.agents/skills/ios-app-manager`\n36|- Symlinks binary to `~/.local/bin/ios-app-manager`\n37|- `--select-xcode` runs only a hardcoded privileged list: `sudo -v` and `sudo xcode-select -s /Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer`\n38|\n39|## Repository structure\n40|\n41|```\n42|skill-ios-app-manager/\n43|├── SKILL.md              # This file (skill definition)\n44|├── blueprints/           # Versioned example relux-feature blueprints\n45|├── references/           # CLI, DSL, workflow docs\n46|├── diagrams/             # Architecture diagrams (PlantUML)\n47|├── scripts/setup.sh      # Global skill installer\n48|└── tuist-starter/        # Go CLI source\n49|    ├── cmd/, internal/, pkg/\n50|    ├── go.mod, Makefile\n51|    └── testdata/\n52|```\n53|\n54|## Native macOS\n55|\n56|For native macOS apps and menu-bar utilities use the dedicated `generate macos-app` plugin. Read [references/native-macos.md](references/native-macos.md). Do not use the iOS init pipeline and patch its generated platform settings.\n57|\n58|## Quick start\n59|\n60|1. Initialize from config:\n61|   - `ios-app-manager init --config ios-app-manager.json --output .`\n62|2. Generate helper targets:\n63|   - `ios-app-manager generate makefile`\n64|   - `ios-app-manager generate project-config`\n65|3. Run local cycle:\n66|   - `make setup && make build && make test`\n67|\n68|Use `--force` when you intentionally want to overwrite scaffold files:\n69|- `ios-app-manager init --config ios-app-manager.json --output . --force`\n70|\n71|## Scaffold Change Policy\n72|\n73|Follow this policy when changing the scaffold itself or when a project needs scaffold-owned output that the current CLI cannot express.\n74|\n75|Do not manually patch files owned by `ios-app-manager` scaffolding or generators (`Project.swift`, `Workspace.swift`, `Package.swift`, `Tuist/ProjectDescriptionHelpers/*`, generated app configuration, module scaffolds) when a required capability is missing from the tool.\n76|\n77|If the CLI cannot express the needed setup, document the scaffold gap first: the blocked user intent, the generated files that would otherwise need changes, and the `ios-app-manager` command/generator that should own the behavior. Then decide whether to extend `ios-app-manager`; do not hide the gap with one-off scaffold edits.\n78|\n79|Scaffold extensions must follow the plugin architecture. If the behavior belongs to an existing scaffold/setup plugin, extend that plugin. If it is a distinct scaffold concern, add a new pluggable scaffold module/generator and register it through the relevant registry instead of wiring ad hoc logic into unrelated commands.\n80|\n81|For broad scaffold domains, keep the top-level plugin as an orchestrator and put concrete concerns into subplugins. Example: `generate app-capabilities` owns the host app capabilities domain, while `app-groups` is a capability subplugin with `init` dependency; future capabilities must be added as separate subplugins rather than collected into one large capability function.\n82|\n83|The same plugin/subplugin rule applies to test targets and app extensions:\n84|- `test-targets` is the orchestration plugin; unit-test-target and UI-test-target behavior belongs in separate subplugins with explicit target-name inputs.\n85|- `app-extensions` owns only shared extension infrastructure and registry contracts.\n86|- Every concrete extension type is its own plugin, for example widget-base/static-widget/live-activity/notification-service.\n87|- Do not collect unrelated test target or extension target behavior into one large generator.\n88|\n89|Extension targets must keep testable internals out of the `.appex` wrapper. A concrete extension plugin must generate a thin extension target plus a dedicated SwiftPM package named `<ExtensionName>Core`; business logic, payload parsing, timeline/content builders, attachment handling, and other internals go into that Core package. The extension target links the Core product and owns only the platform runtime entrypoint glue. Tests should target the Core package or a generated unit-test target, not the `.appex` target directly.\n90|\n91|Scaffold code, templates, docs, and tests must stay organization-agnostic. Use generic examples such as `com.example.*` / `group.com.example.*`; the only non-generic organization reference allowed in this skill is Relux Works ownership/module metadata. Do not add external organization, client, product, Jira, GitLab, or project-specific identifiers to scaffold sources.\n92|\n93|Scaffold plugins and subplugins must be idempotent. Re-running the same command against the same config must not duplicate Swift declarations, manifest entries, generated files, dependencies, entitlements, or package settings. When config values change, the plugin should converge generated output to the current config rather than append another variant.\n94|\n95|## Command groups\n96|\n97|### Project lifecycle\n98|\n99|- Init scaffold: `ios-app-manager init --config <path> --output <dir> [--force]`\n100|- Generate Makefile: `ios-app-manager generate makefile`\n101|- Generate SwiftLint config: `ios-app-manager generate swiftlint`\n102|- Sync project manifest config: `ios-app-manager generate project-config`\n103|- Sync generic app runtime config: `ios-app-manager generate application-configuration`\n104|- Sync typed distribution/backend runtime policy: `ios-app-manager generate runtime-profiles`\n105|- Sync host app capabilities from config: `ios-app-manager generate app-capabilities`\n106|- Generate app/extension bundle ids: `ios-app-manager generate bundle-id`\n107|- Generate app/extension versions: `ios-app-manager generate versions`\n108|- Synchronize Tuist and package min target: `ios-app-manager generate min-target`\n109|- Generate app/extension signing team id: `ios-app-manager generate team-id`\n110|- Generate host app background modes config: `ios-app-manager generate background-modes-config`\n111|- Generate host app theme/orientation presentation config: `ios-app-manager generate presentation-config`\n112|- Generate host app export compliance config: `ios-app-manager generate export-compliance-config`\n113|- Generate host app privacy usage descriptions config: `ios-app-manager generate privacy-usage-descriptions-config`\n114|- Generate app/extension Swift strictness: `ios-app-manager generate build-flags`\n115|- Generate root/module package strictness: `ios-app-manager generate package-strictness`\n116|- Clean artifacts: `ios-app-manager clean [--deep] [--kill-xcode]`\n117|- Status: `ios-app-manager status`\n118|- Diagram: `ios-app-manager diagram` — generates PlantUML module dependency diagram\n119|- Build profile: `ios-app-manager profile build`\n120|- Rendered layout helper: `ios-app-manager profile layout scaffold`\n121|- Rendered layout XML analysis: `ios-app-manager profile layout analyze --input <xml-or-log>`\n122|- Runtime profile helper: `ios-app-manager profile runtime scaffold`\n123|- Runtime profile log analysis: `ios-app-manager profile runtime analyze --input <log>`\n124|- Runtime error log analysis: `ios-app-manager profile runtime errors [--input <log>]`\n125|- Add test targets: `ios-app-manager test-targets setup --unit-target <UnitTestsName> --ui-target <UITestsName>`\n126|\n127|Generate commands are scaffold generator plugins:\n128|- Each `generate <artifact>` entrypoint is a separate scaffold plugin with its own responsibility and dependency contract.\n129|- Use this pattern for scaffold-only sync tasks instead of overloading `init`.\n130|- `generate project-config` is the orchestration entrypoint for project manifest sync and currently runs `generate bundle-id`, `generate versions`, `generate min-target`, `generate team-id`, `generate platform-destinations`, `generate background-modes-config`, `generate presentation-config`, `generate export-compliance-config`, `generate privacy-usage-descriptions-config`, `generate application-configuration`, `generate runtime-profiles`, `generate app-capabilities`, `generate build-flags`, and `generate package-strictness`.\n131|- `generate bundle-id` depends on the `init` scaffold shape and syncs `bundle_id` from `ios-app-manager.json` into the host app `Project.swift` and every `Extensions/*/Project.swift`. Extensions keep their own suffixes, while the containing host bundle root converges to the configured app bundle id.\n132|- `generate versions` depends on the `init` scaffold shape and syncs both `marketing_version` and `project_version` from `ios-app-manager.json` into the host app `Project.swift` and every `Extensions/*/Project.swift`.\n133|- `generate min-target` depends on the same scaffold shape and synchronizes `min_target` across every generated host/test/extension Tuist target, existing root `Package.swift` `IPHONEOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET` overrides, and first-party local-package iOS `platforms` declarations recursively discovered below `modules_path`. Local package declarations converge to PackageDescription-compatible `.iOS(\"<major>.<minor>\")` string syntax, accepting older enum declarations as input. It validates all target layouts and writability before writing, so unsupported declarations and partial layouts do not leave a partial migration.\n134|- `generate team-id` depends on the same scaffold shape and syncs `team_id` into `developmentTeam` constants and `DEVELOPMENT_TEAM` build settings for the host app, app-like targets, test targets, and extensions.\n135|- `generate background-modes-config` depends on the same scaffold shape and syncs host app `background_modes` values into `UIBackgroundModes`. Values are validated against Apple's documented `UIBackgroundModes` strings; common values include `audio` for Audio, AirPlay, and Picture in Picture, `remote-notification` for APNs background notification delivery, and explicit `voip`. Empty or omitted values remove the scaffold-owned key.\n136|- `generate presentation-config` depends on the same scaffold shape and syncs host app presentation keys from `theme` and `orientation` in `ios-app-manager.json`. Supported values: `theme` is `automatic`, `light`, or `dark`; `orientation` is `automatic`, `portrait`, or `landscape`. Automatic values remove the owned Info.plist keys and let iOS use defaults.\n137|- `generate export-compliance-config` depends on the same scaffold shape and syncs host app export compliance from `uses_non_exempt_encryption` in `ios-app-manager.json`. An explicit `false` writes `ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption` as `.boolean(false)`; omitting the field removes the owned Info.plist key.\n138|- `generate privacy-usage-descriptions-config` depends on the same scaffold shape and syncs host app `privacy_usage_descriptions` values into Info.plist usage description strings such as `NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription`, `NSBluetoothPeripheralUsageDescription`, `NSCameraUsageDescription`, `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription`, and `NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription`. Empty values remove scaffold-owned keys.\n139|- `generate application-configuration` depends on the same scaffold shape and syncs product-level runtime identity from `ios-app-manager.json` into an `ApplicationConfiguration` Info.plist dictionary for the host app, app-like targets, and extensions. It also generates the `SharedConfig` reader source and app-target `Configuration+ApplicationConfiguration.swift` facade. This dictionary is distinct from target identity keys such as `CFBundleIdentifier`.\n140|- `generate runtime-profiles` owns typed distribution/backend descriptors, fail-closed Firebase public-input validation, explicit public-identity sharing groups, Tuist configurations/schemes, executable Tests actions, and policy-aware AppConfig integration. `runtime_profiles.test_action.targets` must map at least one existing test target; optional static non-secret `launch_arguments` are emitted only for the Tests action. Read [`references/runtime-profiles.md`](references/runtime-profiles.md) before adding or changing runtime-profile config.\n141|- `generate app-capabilities` depends on the same scaffold shape and orchestrates host app capability subplugins.\n142|- `app-groups` is an app capability subplugin with `init` dependency; it syncs configured `app_groups` into `Tuist/ProjectDescriptionHelpers/AppCapabilities.swift`, host/test target `Project.swift` Info.plist `AppGroups` dictionaries, generated `SharedConfig`, and `Configuration+AppGroups.swift`. Generated app-group code reads product-level service identity through `Configuration.ApplicationConfiguration`, so prefer `generate project-config` when syncing app groups.\n143|- `generate build-flags` depends on the same scaffold shape and syncs Swift language, strict memory safety, strict concurrency, approachable concurrency, default actor isolation, and upcoming-feature restriction settings from `project_settings.swift` into the host app and extensions.\n144|- `generate package-strictness` syncs the same `project_settings.swift` Swift language/concurrency settings into root `Package.swift` and every module `Packages/*/Package.swift`.\n145|- When `project_settings.swift` is omitted, Swift strictness defaults are derived from `swift_version` and the scaffold's current strict baseline.\n146|- Generated Makefiles use `tuist generate --no-open` by default. To auto-open Xcode explicitly, run `tuist generate --open` yourself or override the generated Makefile call with `make generate TUIST_GENERATE_FLAGS=--open`.\n147|- Generated `make build` and `make test` run `tuist generate` before `xcodebuild` and reuse `make clean-package-artifacts` on exit. The cleanup hook is a no-op by default (`PACKAGE_ARTIFACT_CLEANUP_CMD ?= :`); projects that need checkout-specific cleanup should override that variable in the Makefile custom section. Build uses a generic iOS Simulator destination by default, while test keeps the configured concrete simulator destination unless overridden.\n148|- Projects may configure lifecycle scripts in `ios-app-manager.json` under `scripts.pre_tuist_generate`. Each script node has `path`, `language`, and optional `description`; paths are relative to the project root and cannot escape it. Supported `language` values are `bash`, `swift`, `go`, and `executable`. Generated Makefiles run these scripts after `tuist install` and before `tuist generate`, which is the safe point for patching SwiftPM checkouts before Tuist converts them into Xcode projects.\n149|\n150|App Groups capability contract:\n151|- Configure groups in `ios-app-manager.json` with `app_groups`. Configure the generated shared config package name with `shared_config.module_name`; it defaults to `SharedConfig`.\n152|- `app-groups` emits entitlement declarations, root `Package.swift` dependency/product type entries, app/test `.external(name: \"<shared_config.module_name>\")` dependencies, generated shared package sources, and app/test Info.plist values.\n153|- Info.plist values are grouped under one dictionary key: `\"AppGroups\": .dictionary([...])`. Do not emit root-level `APP_GROUP_*` keys for current scaffold output.\n154|- The dictionary key for each app group is derived from the identifier, not hardcoded. `group.<bundle_id>` maps to `main`; `group.<bundle_id>.<suffix>` maps from `<suffix>`; other `group.*` identifiers drop only the `group.` prefix.\n155|- Dictionary keys are sanitized to lowerCamelCase Swift identifiers and reused as `AppGroupSlot.dictionaryKey` in the generated shared package. Validation must reject collisions between generated keys.\n156|- Example: with `bundle_id = \"com.example.demo.app\"`, `group.com.example.demo.app.shared` maps to `AppGroups.shared`, and `group.com.example.demo.app.sso` maps to `AppGroups.sso`.\n157|- App code, tests, and extensions should read app groups through generated APIs such as `DemoAppGroups.read(from:)`; do not hardcode group identifiers or duplicate dictionary-key derivation in handwritten code.\n158|- App-group service identity should come from generated `Configuration.ApplicationConfiguration.current.applicationBundleIdentifier`, not duplicated `bundle_id` constants in app code.\n159|\n160|Project config sync workflow:\n161|```bash\n162|# 1. bump project config in ios-app-manager.json\n163|$EDITOR ios-app-manager.json\n164|\n165|# 2. restick manifest config into app + extensions + packages\n166|ios-app-manager generate project-config\n167|\n168|# 3. regenerate Tuist project artifacts\n169|tuist generate --no-open\n170|```\n171|\n172|Swift strictness lives in `ios-app-manager.json` under `project_settings.swift`, for example:\n173|```json\n174|{\n175|  \"project_settings\": {\n176|    \"swift\": {\n177|      \"language_mode\": \"v6\",\n178|      \"strict_memory_safety\": \"yes\",\n179|      \"concurrency\": {\n180|        \"approachable\": false,\n181|        \"default_actor_isolation\": \"nonisolated\",\n182|        \"strict_checking\": \"complete\",\n183|        \"member_import_visibility\": \"yes\",\n184|        \"existential_any\": \"yes\"\n185|      }\n186|    }\n187|  }\n188|}\n189|```\n190|\n191|Leaf workflows remain available:\n192|```bash\n193|ios-app-manager generate versions\n194|ios-app-manager generate min-target\n195|ios-app-manager generate team-id\n196|ios-app-manager generate background-modes-config\n197|ios-app-manager generate presentation-config\n198|ios-app-manager generate export-compliance-config\n199|ios-app-manager generate privacy-usage-descriptions-config\n200|ios-app-manager generate application-configuration\n201|ios-app-manager generate runtime-profiles\n202|ios-app-manager generate app-capabilities\n203|ios-app-manager generate build-flags\n204|ios-app-manager generate package-strictness\n205|```\n206|\n207|### Profile diagnostics\n208|\n209|Use `profile` when an agent needs evidence about build time, runtime slowness, startup latency, runtime failures, or the UI hierarchy that actually rendered on simulator/device.\n210|\n211|Decision table:\n212|\n213|| Question | Command | Primary output |\n214|| --- | --- | --- |\n215|| Why is the build slow? | `ios-app-manager profile build` | slow commands, target work, critical path estimate |\n216|| What did an existing build log spend time on? | `ios-app-manager profile build --log <xcodebuild.log> --skip-graph` | parsed timing summary without rebuilding |\n217|| Which SwiftUI/function calls are slow or repeated? | `ios-app-manager profile runtime scaffold` then `profile runtime analyze --input <log>` | `IAM_PROFILE` aggregation |\n218|| How long until first meaningful render? | `PerformanceProbe.markAppStart()` + `.firstRenderProfiled(\"RootView\")` then `profile runtime analyze` | app-start-to-first-render duration |\n219|| What runtime errors/faults happened? | `ios-app-manager profile runtime errors [--input <log>]` | grouped errors, crash/exception/hang hints |\n220|| What UI hierarchy actually rendered? | `ios-app-manager profile layout scaffold` then `profile layout analyze --input <xml-or-log>` | rendered accessibility XML tree and layout issues |\n221|\n222|Command map:\n223|\n224|```bash\n225|# Build timing + target graph critical path\n226|ios-app-manager profile build\n227|ios-app-manager profile build --jobs 8 --configuration Debug\n228|ios-app-manager profile build --log .temp/build-profile/xcodebuild.log --skip-graph\n229|\n230|# Runtime view/function/startup instrumentation\n231|ios-app-manager profile runtime scaffold\n232|ios-app-manager profile runtime analyze --input .temp/runtime-profile.log\n233|\n234|# Runtime errors/faults/crash hints\n235|ios-app-manager profile runtime errors --input .temp/runtime-errors.log\n236|ios-app-manager profile runtime errors --simulator --device booted --process DemoApp\n237|\n238|# Rendered UI hierarchy for agents\n239|ios-app-manager profile layout scaffold\n240|ios-app-manager profile layout analyze --input .temp/layout/feed.xml\n241|ios-app-manager profile layout analyze --input .temp/layout/ui-test.log --format json\n242|```\n243|\n244|Feature contracts:\n245|\n246|| Feature | Instrumentation | Input | Diagnostics |\n247|| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n248|| Build profiling | none required | `xcodebuild -showBuildTimingSummary`, Tuist `legacyJSON` graph | top commands, target work, ideal parallelism ceiling, critical path estimate |\n249|| Runtime profiling | generated `PerformanceProbe.swift` in app target | app logs containing `IAM_PROFILE` JSON lines | counts, total/average/max duration, main-thread slow calls, repeated-call warnings |\n250|| Startup timing | `PerformanceProbe.markAppStart()` and `.firstRenderProfiled(...)` | same `IAM_PROFILE` log | app-start-to-first-render duration |\n251|| Runtime errors | optional `PerformanceProbe.error(...)`; unified logs also supported | `IAM_ERROR`, unified-log NDJSON/plain text | grouped signatures and hints for crash, exception, hang, thread checker, SwiftUI background publish |\n252|| Rendered layout XML | generated `LayoutHierarchyProbe.swift` in UI test target | XCTest XML, Appium/WDA page source, or `IAM_LAYOUT_XML_*` log block | agent-readable tree, duplicate identities, missing interactive identity, tiny tap targets, offscreen frames |\n253|\n254|Runtime instrumentation usage:\n255|\n256|```swift\n257|@main\n258|struct DemoApp: App {\n259|    init() {\n260|        PerformanceProbe.markAppStart()\n261|    }\n262|\n263|    var body: some Scene {\n264|        WindowGroup {\n265|            RootView()\n266|                .profiled(\"RootView\")\n267|                .firstRenderProfiled(\"RootView\")\n268|        }\n269|    }\n270|}\n271|\n272|let items = PerformanceProbe.measure(\"Feed.filter\") {\n273|    allItems.filter(\\.isVisible)\n274|}\n275|```\n276|\n277|Rendered layout usage:\n278|\n279|```swift\n280|final class FeedUITests: XCTestCase {\n281|    @MainActor\n282|    func testFeedLayoutDump() {\n283|        let app = XCUIApplication()\n284|        app.launch()\n285|        attachLayoutHierarchyXML(app, name: \"feed\", screenName: \"Feed\")\n286|    }\n287|}\n288|```\n289|\n290|Artifacts and references:\n291|\n292|| Artifact | Location |\n293|| --- | --- |\n294|| Build profiling research | `references/build-profiling-research.md` |\n295|| Runtime profiling research | `references/runtime-profiling-research.md` |\n296|| Runtime error diagnostics research | `references/runtime-error-diagnostics-research.md` |\n297|| Rendered layout hierarchy research | `references/layout-hierarchy-diagnostics-research.md` |\n298|| Architecture overview | `references/profile-diagnostics-architecture.md` |\n299|| Architecture diagram | `diagrams/profile-diagnostics-architecture.puml` |\n300|\n301|Implementation notes:\n302|\n303|- `scripts/setup.sh` verifies Go and Tuist, then reports Xcode/profile-tool readiness for `xcodebuild`, `xcrun/simctl`, macOS `log`, and optional `xctrace`; missing Xcode tools are warnings so the CLI can still be installed.\n304|- `./scripts/setup.sh --select-xcode` is the only setup path that uses sudo; it prints the exact hardcoded command list first and does not evaluate user-provided shell strings.\n305|- Build profiling is local-first and works without Tuist Cloud.\n306|- Runtime helpers are debug-only and use public APIs/signposts plus structured log lines.\n307|- Rendered layout diagnostics use XCTest/accessibility hierarchy, not private UIKit/SwiftUI view graph scraping.\n308|- Prefer JSON output when another agent or CI step will consume a report.\n309|\n310|### Physical iOS launch with logs\n311|\n312|When a user asks to start the app on all connected physical iPhones/iPads, with or without rebuild, and keep runtime logs attached, use the workflow in [`references/physical-ios-launch-log-workflow.md`](references/physical-ios-launch-log-workflow.md).\n313|\n314|Key rules:\n315|- Keep the workflow generic: discover USB devices or accept explicit UDIDs; never bake local device names or identifiers into reusable scripts.\n316|- Put project-specific app defaults, bundle id, environment, and launch flags in the generated Makefile's preserved custom section.\n317|- Start log capture before launching the app so startup, Bluetooth, and permission/runtime events are not missed.\n318|- Keep the capture command in the foreground until the user says to stop, then terminate child log streams and leave raw plus filtered artifacts under `.temp/`.\n319|- Use `devicectl device process launch` for CoreDevice-compatible devices and `ios-deploy --bundle <app> --noinstall --justlaunch` as a legacy fallback when CoreDevice cannot launch a connected device.\n320|\n321|### Infrastructure setup (run in order)\n322|\n323|- IoC container: `ios-app-manager ioc setup`\n324|- Relux state management: `ios-app-manager relux setup`\n325|- Keychain wrapper: `ios-app-manager secure-store setup --access-group <group>`\n326|- Token provider: `ios-app-manager token-provider setup`\n327|- Utilities: `ios-app-manager utilities setup`\n328|- FoundationPlus (re-export + helpers): `ios-app-manager foundation-plus setup`\n329|- SwiftUIPlus (re-export + components): `ios-app-manager swiftui-plus setup`\n330|- Test targets: `ios-app-manager test-targets setup --unit-target <UnitTestsName> --ui-target <UITestsName>`\n331|- App extensions base: `ios-app-manager app-extensions setup`\n332|- Notification Service Extension: `ios-app-manager notification-service setup [--extension-target <Name>] [--bundle-id-suffix <suffix>]`\n333|- Widget base (WidgetBundle + WidgetKit): `ios-app-manager widget-base setup`\n334|- App Intents (interactive widgets): `ios-app-manager app-intents setup`\n335|- Static widget (timeline widget): `ios-app-manager static-widget setup`\n336|- Live Activity (ActivityKit + Dynamic Island): `ios-app-manager live-activity setup`\n337|- HTTP client: `ios-app-manager http-client setup`\n338|- AppConfig (env switching + ApiConfigurator): `ios-app-manager app-config setup`\n339|- Firebase REST auth Relux module: `ios-app-manager fireauth-relux setup`\n340|\n341|Pipeline order matters — each command has prerequisites:\n342|```\n343|init → ioc → relux → utilities\n344|     → foundation-plus → swiftui-plus → test-targets\n345|     → app-extensions → notification-service\n346|                     → widget-base → app-intents → static-widget\n347|                                  → live-activity\n348|     → module create (blueprints)\n349|     → secure-store → token-provider → app-config → fireauth-relux → http-client\n350|```\n351|\n352|### Module management\n353|\n354|- Create module: `ios-app-manager module create <name> --type <feature|kit|shared|ui|utility>`\n355|- Create relux module from blueprint: `ios-app-manager module create --from <blueprint.json>`\n356|- Generate blueprint template: `ios-app-manager module blueprint <Name>`\n357|- List modules: `ios-app-manager module list`\n358|- Delete module: `ios-app-manager module delete <name> [--force]`\n359|\n360|Versioned blueprint examples:\n361|- [`blueprints/xflow-ios/Auth.blueprint.json`](blueprints/xflow-ios/Auth.blueprint.json)\n362|- [`blueprints/xflow-ios/CRM.blueprint.json`](blueprints/xflow-ios/CRM.blueprint.json)\n363|- [`blueprints/xflow-ios/Onboarding.blueprint.json`](blueprints/xflow-ios/Onboarding.blueprint.json)\n364|- [`blueprints/xflow-ios/Organizations.blueprint.json`](blueprints/xflow-ios/Organizations.blueprint.json)\n365|- [`blueprints/xflow-ios/Profile.blueprint.json`](blueprints/xflow-ios/Profile.blueprint.json)\n366|\n367|Module type guidance:\n368|- `feature`: UI module, interface/implementation split (no Relux business logic)\n369|- `relux-feature`: Blueprint-only. Use `module blueprint <Name>` to generate template, then `module create --from <file>.blueprint.json`\n370|- `kit`: Business logic library with interface/implementation split\n371|- `shared`: Shared state/services with interface/implementation split\n372|- `ui`: Pure UI components with interface/implementation split\n373|- `utility`: Single-package utility module (no interface/impl split)\n374|\n375|### Dependency management\n376|\n377|- Internal add: `ios-app-manager dep add <module> --depends-on <other>`\n378|- Internal remove: `ios-app-manager dep remove <module> --depends-on <other>`\n379|- External add: `ios-app-manager dep add-external --url <git_url> --version <ver> [--module <target>] [--product <product>] [--target-setting KEY=VALUE] [--app-target]`\n380|- External remove: `ios-app-manager dep remove-external --package <name>`\n381|- List dependencies: `ios-app-manager dep list [<module>]`\n382|\n383|Framework policy:\n384|- Scaffold-generated local package products are emitted as dynamic libraries by default.\n385|- External Swift package products added through `setup` plugins, `module create --from <blueprint>`, or `dep add-external` are automatically forced to `.framework` in root `Package.swift` under Tuist `PackageSettings.productTypes`. Use `--product` when the Swift product name differs from the package name. Use `--target-setting KEY=VALUE` for product-level Tuist `PackageSettings.targetSettings` overrides such as package min-target fixes.\n386|- `dep remove-external` also removes the matching framework override from root `Package.swift`.\n387|- If someone adds a remote package by hand outside `ios-app-manager`, this policy is not applied until the manifest is brought back through the tool flow or patched manually.\n388|\n389|### Entitlements\n390|\n391|- List keys: `ios-app-manager entitlements list`\n392|- Optional explicit plist path: `--path <entitlements_file>`\n393|\n394|### Push tooling\n395|\n396|- Get token: `ios-app-manager push token`\n397|- Send push: `ios-app-manager push send --token <token> [--env dev|prod] [--payload <file>]`\n398|\n399|### DSL entrypoints\n400|\n401|- Query: `ios-app-manager q '<query>'`\n402|- Mutation: `ios-app-manager m '<mutation>'`\n403|\n404|### Diagnostics\n405|\n406|- Diagram: `ios-app-manager diagram` — generates PlantUML dependency graph of all modules in the project\n407|\n408|## Scaffolding pipeline — dependency graph\n409|\n410|See [`diagrams/scaffolding-pipeline.puml`](diagrams/scaffolding-pipeline.puml) for the visual dependency graph. Always consult this diagram to understand what depends on what before running setup commands.\n411|\n412|### Pipeline elements\n413|\n414|| Element | CLI command | What it creates | Prerequisites |\n415||---------|-------------|-----------------|---------------|\n416|| **init** | `init` | Project scaffold: Tuist manifests, App.swift, Info.plist, entitlements, Configuration, Assets | Config file |\n417|| **ioc** | `ioc setup` | SwiftIoC integration: Registry.swift, App.swift init injection, SwiftIoC dependency | init |\n418|| **relux** | `relux setup` | Relux state management: ReluxLogger, Registry infra, swift-relux + swiftui-relux deps | ioc |\n419|| **secure-store** | `secure-store setup --access-group <group>` | SecureStore + SecureStoreImpl plus a focused, non-destructive Registry patch | ioc |\n420|| **token-provider** | `token-provider setup` | TokenProvider + TokenProviderImpl: token storage/refresh | ioc |\n421|| **fireauth-relux** | `fireauth-relux setup` | Exact FireAuthRelux/FireAuthKit pins, generated Firebase REST loader, environment-scoped durable session store, focused Registry registration/Relux wrapper, and in-process/app-process deterministic test selection | relux, token-provider, app-config, secure-store |\n422|| **utilities** | `utilities setup` | Utilities single-package: HttpClientUtils helpers | ioc |\n423|| **foundation-plus** | `foundation-plus setup` | FoundationPlus single-package: `@_exported import Foundation`, MaybeData, CompletionStatus | ioc |\n424|| **swiftui-plus** | `swiftui-plus setup` | SwiftUIPlus single-package: `@_exported import SwiftUI`, AsyncButton | ioc |\n425|| **test-targets** | `test-targets setup --unit-target <name> --ui-target <name>` | Unit/UI test target scaffold via unit-test-target and UI-test-target subplugins | init |\n426|| **app-extensions** | `app-extensions setup` | SharedKit package + Extensions/ directory for extension targets; owns shared contracts, not concrete extension internals | init |\n427|| **notification-service** | `notification-service setup [--extension-target <name>] [--bundle-id-suffix <suffix>]` | Notification Service Extension wrapper plus `<ExtensionName>Core` handler package | app-extensions |\n428|| **widget-base** | `widget-base setup` | Widget extension target, thin WidgetBundle wrapper, WidgetKit SDK, App Groups, `<WidgetName>Core` package | app-extensions |\n429|| **app-intents** | `app-intents setup` | AppIntent scaffold in WidgetCore, AppIntents SDK on extension | widget-base |\n430|| **static-widget** | `static-widget setup` | StaticConfiguration widget internals in WidgetCore: TimelineProvider, entry, view with interactive Button(intent:) | widget-base, app-intents |\n431|| **live-activity** | `live-activity setup` | ActivityAttributes in SharedKit, ActivityConfiguration + Dynamic Island in WidgetCore, LiveActivityManager in app target | widget-base |\n432|| **module create** | `module create <Name> --type <type>` | Feature/kit/shared/ui/utility module with file layout, Registry re-generation | ioc |\n433|| **http-client** | `http-client setup` | HttpClient IoC registration, swift-httpclient dep, Configuration constants | ioc |\n434|| **app-config** | `app-config setup` | 8 AppConfig files: Env, Configuration, Manager, ApiConfigurator. Registry IoC patch | ioc, secure-store |\n435|| **diagram** | `diagram` | PlantUML dependency graph of all project modules | init |\n436|\n437|### Important: ordering constraints\n438|\n439|Commands that directly patch Registry.swift (`http-client setup`, `app-config setup`, `token-provider setup`, `fireauth-relux setup`) must run **after** all `module create` calls, because `module create` regenerates Registry from template and wipes direct patches.\n440|\n441|`secure-store setup` and `token-provider setup` patch only their imports,\n442|registration, and builder when Registry.swift already exists. `fireauth-relux\n443|setup` additionally replaces only the named Relux resolver line with a managed\n444|wrapper; the original Relux builder body remains untouched. Unsupported Relux\n445|registration shapes fail explicitly. Do not rerun `ioc setup` merely to adopt\n446|these capabilities in a mature custom composition root.\n447|\n448|FireAuth setup requires validated runtime profiles, generated\n449|`RuntimeProfiles.swift`, and the configured Firebase validation hooks. It reads\n450|each input plist only for validation and never retains its path or API key. The\n451|generated loader resolves the selected descriptor's plist resource, checks its\n452|public project/app/bundle metadata, and otherwise returns FireAuth's explicit\n453|unconfigured state. Live composition stores the session in a Keychain account\n454|derived from the descriptor's auth namespace and stores only a namespace-bound\n455|hashed UID continuity marker in UserDefaults. A provisioned identity with a\n456|missing, corrupt, or mismatched session routes to explicit recovery. Unit or\n457|hosted tests in the app process may install\n458|`installFireAuthReluxModuleFactoryForTesting` before Registry configuration and\n459|must reset it during cleanup. XCUITest runs in a separate process: generated UI\n460|test sources expose `GeneratedFireAuthReluxTestLaunch` arguments/environment,\n461|and the generated App.swift bootstrap selects a deterministic unconfigured\n462|module with a transport that rejects all network requests before the existing\n463|`Registry.configure(...)` call.\n464|\n465|Full recommended pipeline:\n466|```bash\n467|ios-app-manager init\n468|ios-app-manager ioc setup\n469|ios-app-manager relux setup\n470|ios-app-manager utilities setup\n471|ios-app-manager foundation-plus setup\n472|ios-app-manager swiftui-plus setup\n473|ios-app-manager test-targets setup --unit-target <UnitTestsName> --ui-target <UITestsName>\n474|ios-app-manager app-extensions setup\n475|ios-app-manager notification-service setup\n476|ios-app-manager widget-base setup\n477|ios-app-manager app-intents setup\n478|ios-app-manager static-widget setup\n479|ios-app-manager live-activity setup\n480|ios-app-manager module create --from <name>.blueprint.json\n481|ios-app-manager secure-store setup --access-group <group>\n482|ios-app-manager token-provider setup\n483|ios-app-manager app-config setup\n484|ios-app-manager fireauth-relux setup\n485|ios-app-manager http-client setup\n486|ios-app-manager diagram\n487|```\n488|\n489|## Workflow references\n490|\n491|- Command/flag reference: [`references/cli-reference.md`](references/cli-reference.md)\n492|- DSL syntax and operations: [`references/dsl-reference.md`](references/dsl-reference.md)\n493|- End-to-end examples: [`references/workflows.md`](references/workflows.md)\n494|- Dependency diagram: [`diagrams/scaffolding-pipeline.puml`](diagrams/scaffolding-pipeline.puml)\n495|- Blueprint examples: [`blueprints/README.md`](blueprints/README.md)","final_result":true}}}
